The Complete Git Workflow

This is the most important Git diagram.

                    Developer
                        │
                Creates/Edits Files
                        │
                        ▼
              Working Directory
                        │
                 git add
                        ▼
                Staging Area
                        │
               git commit
                        ▼
              Local Repository
                        │
                git push
                        ▼
            Remote Repository
                 (GitHub/Azure Repos)

Every Git command moves changes from one stage to the next.

Step 1: Working Directory

The Working Directory contains your project files.

Example:

project/

README.md

app.py

Dockerfile

main.tf

When you edit a file:

print("Hello Azure")

Git notices the file has changed, but it isn't tracking the new change yet.

Check Status
git status

Example:

Modified:

app.py

The file is modified but unstaged.

Step 2: Staging Area

The Staging Area lets you decide which changes will be included in the next commit.

Example:

Modified files:

README.md

app.py

Dockerfile

Stage only one file:

git add app.py

Now:

Working Directory

README.md

Dockerfile

Staging Area

app.py

Only app.py will be committed.

Stage Everything
git add .

Stages all modified and new files in the current directory.

Why Do We Need a Staging Area?

Imagine you've modified:

README.md

login.py

payment.py

You only finished login.py.

Instead of committing everything:

git add login.py
git commit -m "Added login feature"

The unfinished work stays out of the commit.

This helps keep commits focused and easier to review.

Step 3: Local Repository

After staging:

git commit -m "Added Login API"

Git creates a commit in your local repository.

Local Repository

Commit A

↓

Commit B

↓

Commit C

These commits exist only on your computer until you push them.

Commit Structure

Each commit contains:

Commit ID (SHA)
Author
Date
Commit Message
Snapshot of files

Example:

Commit:
7d8a9f3

Message:
Added Login Feature
View Commit History
git log

Compact view:

git log --oneline

Example:

a12bc34 Added Login

8f9de12 Initial Commit
Step 4: Remote Repository

The remote repository is shared with your team.

Examples:

GitHub
Azure Repos
GitLab
Bitbucket

Push commits:

git push origin main

Now the team can see your changes.

